    <p>I study interdisciplinary affective science and am particularly interested in 
      the psychology of climate change. How do people respond emotionally to problems 
      when the problems are too large and complicated to see or touch? Do the ways that
      we manage those emotions help or hurt? I trained in mindfulness, impulsivity, and
      emotion regulation using psychophysiological, social, and cognitive methods. 
      My questions are increasingly bridging between affective science and environmental 
      psychology.</p>

    <p>I also teach statistics and research methods and mentor students. I particularly
      enjoy working with students who are exploring their intellectual motivations in
      their own studies. This fall I'm teaching a research practicum in emotion 
      regulation and we are conducting an EEG study to probe emotional and motivational 
      signals in the late positive potential.</p>

    <p>There's more about my <a href="/research/">research</a> and <a href="/teaching/">teaching</a> on this site, along with a list of <a href="/publications/">publications</a> and a few open <a href="/resources/">resources</a>.</p>

